note omc: the Omega compiler written in Omega (the om4 subset). Reads Omegese on stdin, writes a runnable
note AArch64 Linux program (or bare function words) on stdout. Built first by om4; its codegen matches om4
note byte for byte on the original subset (seed chain rung 5, ledger L408). Native host operations
note extend that subset; om4 still builds this compiler, and the compiler rebuilds itself identically.
note All compiler state lives in global pools (decision 24: no heap). Every working value is a ulong
note (raw 64 bits) so the language's checked conversions never fire on om4's untyped-register logic.

note ---- the arena (decision 23 as amended 2026-11-11, OMEGESE 9.6; grown at run time since landing 2 of the
note run-time pools, decision 3 of 2026-11-11, docs/RUNTIME-POOLS.md s6). Every table whose size grows with the
note unit (the input, the output, names, long names, functions and their parameters, call sites, trap sites,
note blocks, breaks, value nesting, the x86 fixups, the om2 diag sites) is a region of the one pool a_ar.
note A region starts small and doubles when an index reaches its capacity (a_need), so no table fills.
note a_ar is a run-time pool (global a_ar ulong *, OMEGESE 9.15): its length is 0 when omc starts, and a_need
note enlarges it (a_grow) whenever a group's growth would not fit in the words left: to twice its length, or
note to what the tables need when that is more. So omc has no arena size of its own: the host is the bound.
note When the host refuses (enlarge's negative result, for example under prlimit --as), the arena is compacted
note and the group grows in place to the largest size that fits; when not even one more entry fits, the unit
note is error 14 with an omc-use record on stderr (full=1: the host refused) naming every group and the words
note it held (a_full, a_rec). The static 128 MiB arena this replaces was sized by omc's image under the
note smallest RLIMIT_AS omc runs under (the interplane-forge salvage compile, 268435456) and by the 16777216
note elements of a sized pool (OMEGESE 9.4); a run-time pool is bounded by neither, and the image no longer
note holds the arena.
note a_rb / a_rw: a region's first word and the words it holds; a_rg its group; a_rm its elements per group
note index (0: a byte region, 8 bytes to a word); a_gc / a_gp a group's capacity and the highest index it
note was asked for, plus 1; a_top the words handed out; a_wst the words left behind by moves; a_fl 1 while
note error 14 is the arena's; a_nr the region count; a_wb the staging bytes of read and write.
global a_ar ulong *
note a_cel: the caller-stated memory ceiling C in bytes (OMC_MEMORY_MAX, a_env; 0: none). The arena's bytes A, the
note output's bytes O (written to stdout they are page cache of the same cgroup) and the margin 16 MiB + A / 128
note stay within C (docs/RUNTIME-POOLS.md s6, "The memory ceiling"): a_capw at every growth, a_ceilo before the write
global a_cel ulong 1
note a_rdy: 1 once a_init has given every region its first room (a_need compacts only then, or while region 0 is alone)
global a_rdy ulong 1
note a_hw: the most words a_top ever held (a_hwset); a compaction lowers a_top, not the pages it touched
global a_hw ulong 1
text a_envk "OMC_MEMORY_MAX="
note a_eb: one environment entry (Linux keeps each below 131072 bytes, MAX_ARG_STRLEN)
global a_eb uchar 131072
global a_rb ulong 96
global a_rw ulong 96
global a_rg ulong 96
global a_rm ulong 96
global a_gc ulong 32
global a_gp ulong 32
global a_top ulong 1
global a_wst ulong 1
global a_fl ulong 1
global a_nr ulong 1
note a_ord / a_np: the regions in order of base and their new places, for a_relayout
global a_ord ulong 96
global a_np ulong 96
global a_wb uchar 65536
note nh_b / lh_b / fh_b: the bucket bits of the name, long-name and function hash tables (chained: the
note bucket holds index + 1 of the newest entry, nm_nx / ln_x / fn_nx the one before it in the chain)
global nh_b ulong 1
global lh_b ulong 1
global fh_b ulong 1
note tpend: the first trap site of the function being compiled that no stub was patched to yet (island)
global tpend ulong 1

