note ---- OM-2 section 4 (unroll, inline, folding, bounds, sites). fn_inl/fn_body/fn_bln/fn_pn: a function's
note inline mark, its body offset and line, its parameter names. fstart: where the function being compiled
note starts (no longer a bound: OM-2 functions have no emitted-word budget). nmlo: names below it and at or above nglob are hidden while an
note inlined body is compiled (0 otherwise). fzone: open unrolled bodies and inline frames (folding is
note done only when it is above 0). inl and il_*: the inline frames (at most 4). ilp/nilp: the B words of
note an inlined ret, patched at its end. lastb/tgoc: the word after the last inlined ret's B and the last
note place a branch was patched to. u_*: the unrolled loop of each block. fok/fval/fty: a fold result.
note sitebuf/nsite: the omc-site records of om2 diag.
global fstart ulong 1
global nmlo ulong 1
global fzone ulong 1
global inl ulong 1
global il_cur ulong 8
global il_line ulong 8
global il_ret ulong 8
global il_nn ulong 8
global il_lo ulong 8
global il_held ulong 8
global il_ns ulong 8
global il_res ulong 8
global il_fn ulong 8
global il_p0 ulong 8
global nilp ulong 1
global lastb ulong 1
global tgoc ulong 1
note fuel (E14, E16): the expansion fuel used by the function being compiled: an unrolled loop costs K when
note it starts, an inline expansion 1, at most 65536 in all (error 14)
global fuel ulong 1
global fok ulong 1
global fval ulong 1
global fty ulong 1
global nsite ulong 1
global nie ulong 1

note ---- name table (arena group 3; names in scope bounded by the arena only): packed name, register, type,
note length, kind, address. Lookup is by the chained hash nm_h (group 4) with nm_nx; every change of nn goes
note through nm_set, so a chain holds exactly the names below nn, newest first ----
global nn ulong 1
note ---- long names: tok2 interns each word of 9..63 bytes that is not a number as the packed word
note 1 + 256 * index. Its low byte, 1, is below every byte a word can hold (33..255), so it never equals
note a name of 8 bytes or fewer or a keyword, and two words get the same packed word exactly when they
note are spelled the same: every table that compares packed words takes long names unchanged.
note ln_o: where the spelling starts in the input (it is read there, never copied); ln_l: its length; ln_h
note (group 6) the chained hash, bucket -> index + 1 of the newest, ln_x the one before it; ln_n: the
note count (arena group 5). tw8: the word's first 8 bytes before interning (cp_kind reads
note invariant there). Abstract, layout and layout-field names and IR-mode names stay at 8.
global ln_n ulong 1
global tw8 ulong 1
note ---- OM-2 pinned registers (AArch64 program mode only): X18..X26, which nothing else in a program names,
note hold for the whole run the base address of the first globals and the length of a global whose length
note is no CMP immediate. nm_pb / nm_pl: the base / length register of a global name (0: none); pinv the
note value of pin p (register 18 + p), npin how many are taken. eof_prog sets them before main; scrub sets
note them again after it clears X18..X26. pinshut: 1 once the body pass has begun a function; a global
note declared after that gets no pin, because a scrub compiled before it would clear its register and
note restore only the pins taken so far. ----
global pinv ulong 9
global npin ulong 1
global pinshut ulong 1
note ---- floats in v registers: kind 9 is a float held in v(16 + pool index of x register rv), the twin of
note pool register rv; vsl marks each pool slot whose value lives in its twin (an f32/f64 local name, a
note kind 9 value), saved and loaded as D around a call. fpw asks the next value read for kind 9 (1: a new
note twin, 2 + r: the twin of r); fpo hands that request to the part that makes the value. A float local
note name (nm_fv 1) lives in its twin; its x register holds a copy only while a reader needs one. ----
global vsl uchar 16
global fpw ulong 1
global fpo ulong 1
note fwv: rdval's fpw request at each nesting depth (kept here, not in a register of rdval)

note ---- function table (arena group 7; chained hash fn_h, group 8, with fn_nx): name, address (byte offset
note in out), return type, parameter count, per-parameter type/length/kind (6 each), name length, name offset
note in the input ----
note x86 Linux retains the parser's sixteen logical slots in each native frame.
note No A64 instruction may pass emit once the explicit target is selected (error 25).
global xtarget ulong 1
note xefa: 1 under the first line `target a64efi` (docs/A64-EFI.md): the default AArch64 code generator,
note a PE32+ UEFI image, base-relative addresses (mataddr) and the efi and fw words; xtarget stays 0.
global xefa ulong 1
global xnaddr ulong 1
note x86efi (xtarget 2): exit-site jumps patched to the shared firmware return, the PE bss address,
note and the error/done branch lists of one efi operation (closed by xe 2).
global xnexit ulong 1
global xbss ulong 1
global xenerr ulong 1
global xerrpos ulong 256
global xerrcode ulong 256
global xenend ulong 1
global xendpos ulong 256
global nf ulong 1
global lastfn ulong 1
global scanning ulong 1
global ncalls ulong 1


