note ---- OM-2 section 5 abstract types (16; type 64 + index): name, its length, representation type (4 uint,
note 5 ulong), the preserving operations listed (1 rotr, 2 rotl, 4 and, 8 or, 16 xor), 1 when `holds NAME
note dup32` is declared. aw_*: the word reader of the exact-constructor test (place, packed word, length,
note newlines crossed before it) ----
global ab_name ulong 16
global ab_len ulong 16
global ab_base uchar 16
global ab_ops ulong 16
global ab_holds uchar 16
global nab ulong 1
global aw_p ulong 1
global aw_w ulong 1
global aw_l ulong 1
global aw_nl ulong 1

note ---- OM-2 section 6 unsized views (spec 4 slots, 6, erratum E2). An unsized `view T *` / `edit T *`
note name has length 0 in nm_len and its run-time element count in register nm_lr. Parameters take the
note registers x0..x5 by slots: an unsized array two (pointer, then count), every other parameter one
note (pwid, pslot); psl counts the slots of the fn line being read (6 at most, error 9). ul_k / ul_v: the
note count (kind 1 register / 3 number) of argument i of the call at value depth d, at d * 6 + i ----
global psl ulong 1
note ---- OM-2 section 6 multi-entry units: nexp `export fn` lines (32 at most, function mode only), fn_exp 1
note for an exported function, expf 1 while an `export fn` line is read. With any export, words 0 .. E-1
note of the code are B to each export in source order and the code starts at byte 176 + 4E ----
global nexp ulong 1
global expf ulong 1

note ======== leaf helpers ========

note poolc: the i-th register of the pool x0..x6, x8..x15, x17 (x7 is the runtime, x16 scratch)
fn poolc i ulong to ulong
if lt i 7
ret i
end
if lt i 15
ret add i 1
end
ret 17
end

note pwid: the parameter slots of fn table entry p: 2 for an unsized array (pointer, count), else 1
fn pwid p ulong to ulong
k = ulong uchar get a_ar add get a_rb 37 p
if and or eq k 2 eq k 3 eq get a_ar add get a_rb 36 p 0
ret 2
end
ret 1
end

note unsz: 1 when name entry i is an unsized array (nm_len 0, its count in register nm_lr), else 0. A text
note (kind 5) of length 0 (`text e ""`) is sized: its count is the number 0 and it has no count register.
fn unsz i ulong to ulong
if and eq a_g 6 i 0 ne a_gu 7 i 5
if ne a_gu 7 i 10
ret 1
end
end
ret 0
end

note pslot: the first slot (register) of parameter i of function f (index)
fn pslot f ulong i ulong to ulong
s = ulong 0
j = ulong 0
while lt j i
s = add s pwid add mul f 6 j
j = add j 1
end
ret s
end

note ---- error reporting ----
global msg uchar 65536
global mc ulong 1
note ======== the arena (notes at a_ar). Groups: 0 inp, 1 out, 2 site, 3 names, 4 name hash, 5 long names,
note 6 long-name hash, 7 functions, 8 function hash, 9 calls, 10 trap sites, 11 x86 addresses, 12 x86 exits,
note 13 blocks, 14 breaks, 15 continues, 16 inline rets, 17 inline edges, 18 value depth, 19 and 20 the
note settle order (ksettle). The regions of each group are listed at a_defs. ========

note a_def: region r is in group g with m elements per group index (0: a byte region)
fn a_def r ulong g ulong m ulong to void
put a_rg r g
put a_rm r m
end

note a_words: the words region r takes at group capacity c
fn a_words r ulong c ulong to ulong
m = get a_rm r
if eq m 0
ret shr add c 7 3
end
ret mul c m
end

note a_move: room in region r for group capacity c. The last region handed out grows in place; any other
note moves to the top with its words copied, and the words it leaves behind are counted in a_wst. Words
note past a_top were never handed out and are 0, so new elements start at 0 as a fresh pool's do. No
note room: error 14 (a_full).
fn a_move r ulong c ulong to void
w = a_words r c
o = get a_rw r
if le w o
ret
end
b = get a_rb r
t = get a_top 0
if eq add b o t
if gt sub w o sub len a_ar t
a_full
end
put a_top 0 add b w
put a_rw r w
ret
end
if gt w sub len a_ar t
a_full
end
a_copy t b o
put a_wst 0 add get a_wst 0 o
put a_rb r t
put a_rw r w
put a_top 0 add t w
end

note a_copy: words s .. s + n - 1 of the arena to d .. d + n - 1, in ascending order: d above s + n (no
note overlap, a move to the top) or d below s (a slide down, a_compact)
fn a_copy d ulong s ulong n ulong to void
i = ulong 0
go = true
while go
m = ulong 0
while and go lt m 999999
if ge i n
go = false
else
put a_ar add d i get a_ar add s i
i = add i 1
end
m = add m 1
end
end
end

note a_need: group g is about to use index n. Call sites outside this module test `if ge n get a_gp g`
note first: below the highest index asked so far the capacity already holds n, so no call is made. Below its capacity nothing happens; at or above it every
note region of the group grows to twice the capacity (at least n + 1, at least 64), moving to the top when
note it is not there already. When the moved copies do not fit in the free words, the arena is compacted
note (a_compact: the words moved regions left behind are reclaimed) and the group grows in place
note (a_relayout: everything above its first region slides up), to twice when that fits, else to the
note largest capacity that does (a_gfit). Before that the arena itself grows (a_grow); the compaction runs
note only when the host refused. So a table is refused (error 14) only when the host refuses the arena's
note growth and the live tables with its group grown by one entry no longer fit in the arena as it is.
note a_gp keeps the highest index asked for, plus 1, for the omc-use record.
fn a_need g ulong n ulong to void
if ge n get a_gp g
put a_gp g add n 1
end
c = get a_gc g
if lt n c
ret
end
c = shl c 1
if le c n
c = add n 1
end
if lt c 64
c = ulong 64
end
if gt a_gneed g c sub len a_ar get a_top 0
a_grow a_gneed g c
end
cm = ulong 0
if gt a_gneed g c sub len a_ar get a_top 0
cm = ulong 1
end
if eq a_ok g c add get a_top 0 a_gneed g c 0
cm = ulong 1
end
note under a ceiling a move to the top never raises the high-water (the group grows in place after a compaction),
note and a group never grows to less than its doubling: then the words handed out follow the same course for
note every ceiling, and a unit refused at a ceiling is refused at every lower one
if ne get a_cel 0 0
if ne get a_rdy 0 0
if gt add get a_top 0 a_gneed g c get a_hw 0
cm = ulong 1
end
end
end
if ne cm 0
if eq get a_rdy 0 0
if ne get a_nr 0 1
a_full
end
end
a_compact
if ne get a_cel 0 0
if gt a_gextra g c sub len a_ar get a_top 0
a_grow a_gextra g c
end
if ne get a_rdy 0 0
if ne a_bad g c 0
a_full
end
end
end
if ne a_bad g c 0
c = a_gfit g n c
end
a_relayout g c
put a_gc g c
a_hwset
ret
end
r = ulong 0
while lt r get a_nr 0
if eq get a_rg r g
a_move r c
end
r = add r 1
end
put a_gc g c
a_hwset
end

note a_hwset: a_hw follows a_top up. While a_rdin reads the input, region 0 is the only region (a_nr 1): it grows
note in place, words past the input are handed out but never touched, and how far depends on how the reads were
note cut (a pipe or a file), so a_hw is not followed then; a_rdin sets it to the words the input holds
fn a_hwset to void
if eq get a_nr 0 1
ret
end
if gt get a_top 0 get a_hw 0
put a_hw 0 get a_top 0
end
end

note a_grow: the arena a_ar grows so that w more words are free above a_top: to twice its length, or to a_top
note + w when that is more (doubling, so the copies a_need makes are linear in the arena's final size). A
note refusal by the host (enlarge's negative result: -12 when mmap or mremap fails, -22 past 2^63 bytes)
note leaves a_ar as it was, and a_need goes on to compact. Words past the old length are 0 (a fresh pool's
note pages), as a_move expects of words above a_top.
fn a_grow w ulong to void
t = add get a_top 0 w
n = shl len a_ar 1
if lt n t
n = t
end
note under a memory ceiling the arena grows at most to a_capw words; when that is less than the tables need,
note a_need goes on as after a host refusal (compaction, then error 14 full=1)
if ne get a_cel 0 0
c = a_capw 0
if gt n c
n = c
end
end
r = enlarge a_ar n
end

note a_capw: the arena's largest length in words under the ceiling with the output so far: A + A / 128 + 16 MiB + O
note <= C, A = 8 words, so words <= (C - 16 MiB - O) / 129 * 16 (0 when C does not cover 16 MiB and O)
fn a_capw z ulong to ulong
f = add 16777216 a_resv 0 0
if le get a_cel 0 f
ret 0
end
ret mul div sub get a_cel 0 f 129 16
end

note a_marg: the margin added to b bytes of arena: b / 128 for the kernel's page tables, 16 MiB for omc's image
note (docs/RUNTIME-POOLS.md s6, "The margin")
fn a_marg b ulong to ulong
ret add add b shr b 7 16777216
end

note a_resv: the output reserve in bytes: the capacities of group 1 (the artifact) and group 2 (the om2 diag site
note records written after it), with group g at capacity c. Everything omc writes beyond them is small and
note inside the margin (diagnostics, the omc-use record)
fn a_resv g ulong c ulong to ulong
r = get a_gc 1
if eq g 1
r = c
end
t = get a_gc 2
if eq g 2
t = c
end
ret add r t
end

note a_ok: 1 when group g at capacity c with the words handed out at t keeps the budget under the ceiling: the most
note words ever handed out (a_hw, or t when higher) in bytes, their margin and the output reserve within C; 1
note without a ceiling. Every growth keeps it, so the artifact and the site records always fit when written
fn a_ok g ulong c ulong t ulong to ulong
if eq get a_cel 0 0
ret 1
end
h = get a_hw 0
if gt t h
h = t
end
if gt add a_marg shl h 3 a_resv g c get a_cel 0
ret 0
end
ret 1
end

note a_bad: 1 when group g cannot grow in place to capacity c after a compaction: no room in the arena, or past
note the budget (a_ok)
fn a_bad g ulong c ulong to ulong
x = a_gextra g c
if gt x sub len a_ar get a_top 0
ret 1
end
if eq a_ok g c add get a_top 0 x 0
ret 1
end
ret 0
end

note a_ceilo: before the artifact (o bytes) is written: under a ceiling, the most arena words ever handed out
note (a_hw: the words above it were never handed out and their pages never touched), the margin and o must fit in
note it (the output grows inside its region without a growth to check), else error 14 full=1 and nothing is written
fn a_ceilo o ulong to void
if eq get a_cel 0 0
ret
end
if gt add a_marg shl get a_hw 0 3 o get a_cel 0
a_full
end
end

note a_env: the memory ceiling from the environment (docs/RUNTIME-POOLS.md s6): the first entry named
note OMC_MEMORY_MAX, read with envcnt / envread into a_eb before the input; an entry that does not fit is error 26
